From 3eb2091f78ddc833ce0064d3ae21894e3f83a4d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Fuchs <fuchsfl@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 16:26:23 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] sh: Fix kernel unaligned access fixup in branch delay slots

An unaligned access by an instruction in a branch delay slot is fixed
up by handle_delayslot(). It fetched the delay slot instruction with
copy_from_user(), even when the fault happened in kernel mode. Since
the removal of set_fs() access_ok() rejects kernel addresses, so the
fetch always fails for kernel code and the kernel dies with:

  delay-slot-insn faulting in handle_unaligned_delayslot: 0000 [#1]

The non-delay-slot path in do_address_error() already uses
copy_from_kernel_nofault() for kernel mode. So choose the matching
instruction-fetch accessor from user_mode(regs) and fetch the
instruction.

Fixes: 75d4d29549e5 ("sh: remove CONFIG_SET_FS support")
Signed-off-by: Florian Fuchs <fuchsfl@gmail.com>
---
The issue was hit while using PPP with VJ header compression, where gcc
placed an unaligned checksum store in slhc_compress() [0] into the delay
slot, which paniced the system e.g. using ssh over this line.

I'm preparing another patch for slhc to use the unaligned helpers to
access the TCP checksum. But this fix is still useful to prevent the
die in unaligned fixups, in delay slots that happen in kernel mode or
from trapped io.

[0] https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/4c1367a2d7aad643a6f87c6931b13cc1a25e8ca7/drivers/net/slip/slhc.c#L466

 arch/sh/kernel/traps_32.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/sh/kernel/traps_32.c b/arch/sh/kernel/traps_32.c
index 1271b839a107..a7f20fead5f4 100644
--- a/arch/sh/kernel/traps_32.c
+++ b/arch/sh/kernel/traps_32.c
@@ -287,8 +287,10 @@ static inline int handle_delayslot(struct pt_regs *regs,
 	insn_size_t instruction;
 	void __user *addr = (void __user *)(regs->pc +
 		instruction_size(old_instruction));
+	struct mem_access *insn_ma = user_mode(regs) ?
+		&user_mem_access : &kernel_mem_access;
 
-	if (copy_from_user(&instruction, addr, sizeof(instruction))) {
+	if (insn_ma->from(&instruction, addr, sizeof(instruction))) {
 		/* the instruction-fetch faulted */
 		if (user_mode(regs))
 			return -EFAULT;

